From 065b129c45d87791f791362f775aabf841cfbfa9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 20:41:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] VideoPlayer: the time a read of a local file waits for its
 drive does not end the video

Issue #16, two diagnostics of a user (RTX 5090, films on a USB SSD with UAS): the drive stopped
answering, the kernel aborted its commands after the 30 s SCSI command timeout and reset it
(uas_eh_host_reset_handler success), and Kodi ended the video 1.0 and 1.6 s after the reset with
"Process - eof reading from demuxer". What the diagnostics show:

- the thread of the player was blocked in the kernel (filemap_update_page) for those 30 s, and
  out of it right after the reset (io-timeline of hades-diag, one sample a second);
- the I/O errors of the kernel are those of the readahead requests in flight (flags 0x80700:
  REQ_RAHEAD and REQ_FAILFAST_*, which the block layer does not retry); the page cache then reads
  such pages again with a read of its own, and the drive answered it;
- read(2) did not fail: CFileStreamBuffer logs "Error reading file - assuming eof" for a read that
  fails, and neither log has it; and the player writes "eof reading from demuxer" only when its
  input is not at its end.

The end came from the deadline of the demuxer: ReadInternal() gives libavformat 20 s for a packet
(30 s to open a file), and dvd_file_read() answers AVERROR_EXIT once that deadline has passed. The
read that waited 30 s in the kernel could not be interrupted by it; when it came back with its
data, the next read of the same packet answered AVERROR_EXIT, libavformat's Matroska demuxer took
the error for the end of its input (it resyncs, finds its input at its end and stays done), and
the player ended the video with the drive working again.

Now, for an input that is a local file (CDVDInputStreamFile on a path of this machine), a read
that waits 1 s or more gives that time back to the deadline: the deadline runs again from the end
of the read with what it had left before it (CDemuxReadStall, DemuxReadStall.h). The time the
demuxer spends itself still counts: a file that keeps libavformat busy without a packet still
ends at 20 s. Abort() now also sets a flag that Aborted() answers first, so giving time back
never undoes a stop; it was set only through the deadline, which the next packet set again.
kodi.log says it, a line a minute at most: "CDVDDemuxFFmpeg: a read of <file> waited N ms for its
drive; that wait does not count against the time the demuxer has for a packet ...". Network
inputs and streams keep the deadline as it was.

Host test (demuxstall_test.cpp, the header with Kodi's EndTime): six cases, among them a read that
waits longer than the deadline had (no abort after it, the time left as before the read), a short
read (nothing given back), an abort during the read with and without the flag, and a deadline
that does not run. Without End(), as in RC6, the two cases of a long read fail. What the player
does with the data back (its buffering, the clock) is read in the code, not run; only a drive that
stops answering shows it.
